expect: none
why: both claims the facts carry are named by a test in the change set, almost in the test's own words, so question 3's carried-by-a-test step reaches each of them; the case held a third claim no test carries and called the site none for it, which is why it read 7 of 15 at baseline and why it is split
labelled: 2026-09-22
--- code
export enum Settlement {
  Unsettled = 'unsettled',
  DirectCash = 'direct-cash',
  BrokerCash = 'broker-cash',
  DirectCredit = 'direct-credit',
  BrokerCredit = 'broker-credit',
  EscrowCredit = 'escrow-credit',
}
--- facts
Only pairings that exist in practice are members, so an impossible one like escrow with cash has no name. Members
are ordered by settlement scheme to match the posting catalog, so an account's cells under one scheme sit together
in both.
--- tests
// SettlementOrder.test.ts in the change set
it('orders settlements by scheme so an account\'s cells sit together in the catalog', () => {
  expect(Object.values(Settlement)).toEqual(catalogOrderedSettlements());
});

it('offers escrow only under the credit scheme', () => {
  expect(Object.values(Settlement).filter(isEscrow)).toEqual([Settlement.EscrowCredit]);
});
